  does not preload AGENTS.md. Measured against 2.1.278 on 2026-09-19 with the
  file-reading tools disabled, in a git repository, with `instructionFiles` unset and
  with both of its `agents-md` values -- unloaded in all three. So this file is a
  briefing, not a containment control, and "someone could land an AGENTS.md" is not an
  argument for writing it. The argument is the ordinary one: an agent that has to infer
  the rules from the tree infers them after the first command rather than before it.

  What IS true about AGENTS.md is weaker and still worth knowing: a session reads it,
  unprompted and reliably, the first time it is asked anything about the project. So
  untrusted content there does reach the model -- as a file the agent chose to open,
  which it weighs as a file rather than as instruction.

  THE PROBE THAT SETTLES THIS IS THE POINT, because the obvious one is confounded.
  Drop a marker fact in the candidate file, ask a question only that file answers, and
  a plain `claude -p` will answer correctly whether or not the file was preloaded --
  the agent simply opens it, and in a near-empty probe directory it is the only thing
  to open. Disable the file-reading tools and the two cases separate. Note that
  `--disallowedTools` is variadic, so it eats a positional prompt: pass the prompt on
  stdin. And use a retrieval question, not a behavioural override like "always reply
  X", which the model follows unreliably enough to produce both answers from one
  directory.

  Two things that survive the correction. In a public repo where the content of this
